#!/usr/bin/env bash

#MISE description="Helper functions shared by the build tasks"
#MISE hide=true

# The crate version, which is the single source of truth for everything
# published: the binary self-reports it, and the release workflow checks the tag
# against it. Deliberately NOT duplicated into a second manifest that could
# drift — which is what a package.json here used to be.
crate_version() {
  cargo pkgid | sed -E 's/.*[@#]([0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+.*)$/\1/'
}

# The built binary's filename, the same for every target. It used to be a
# fourth column on the table below, carrying `.exe` for the two Windows rows;
# with those gone it was a constant threaded through two consumers that each had
# to remember to ignore it.
BINARY_NAME="claude-status"

# The supported target, as a `<rust-triple> <os> <cpu>` row. The os/cpu names
# are npm's spelling, kept after `distribution/01` dropped npm because
# `asset_name` already spoke it and renaming it would rename every published
# asset for nothing.
#
# **Apple Silicon macOS, and nothing else.** This table carried six targets
# once, then two, and now one. Each cut was made on evidence rather than taste:
#
#   - six → two (`macos-only`): four of the six had never had a binary
#     *executed* on them by anyone. A local build proved architecture, not
#     execution, so shipping them was shipping a claim nobody checked.
#   - two → one (`release-fix`): the ecosystem stopped serving Intel macOS.
#     pnpm publishes `darwin-arm64`, `linux-*` and `win32-*` and no macOS x64
#     build at any recent version, so CI could not even install its own tooling
#     on an Intel runner — every binary backend fails identically and no backend
#     swap helps. A target whose build tooling has abandoned it is a gap this
#     repo would own indefinitely.
#
#     **That reason has since lapsed and the cut still stands.**
#     `distribution/01` removed pnpm from the toolchain, so an Intel runner has
#     nothing left to fail to install. What did not change is the bar below: a
#     target costs a native `test` runner and a native `build` runner, and
#     contract §9 will not take one back without them. Re-adding darwin-x64 is
#     now a decision about whether it is worth two runners, not a blocked one.
#
# Linux was surveyed before being declined and came back *viable* — credentials
# already degrade off macOS, the crate's platform-specific spots are Unix rather
# than Apple, and TLS pulls in no system library. It costs two native runners, a
# glibc-versus-musl portability floor, and the end of local complete builds: a
# Mac cross-compiles to another Apple slice with a rustup target and cannot
# produce a Linux binary at all. See contract §9.
#
# Everything that COUNTS targets derives from this function — `target_count`,
# `_rust_reassemble`, and the release workflow's collect step, which names both
# the raw asset and the tarball from the row. Two things do NOT, and adding a
# platform back means editing both; nothing here catches a miss:
#
#   1. The `build` AND `test` matrices in `.github/workflows/release.yml` — one
#      runner each, so no artifact reaches a release untested on its own
#      architecture. That rule is why adding a target is expensive, and it is
#      the rule contract §9 says must be met before any target comes back.
#   2. The crate itself, in three places — **Windows will not compile** until
#      every one of them goes back behind a `cfg`, and restoring only the first
#      still fails, after a full CI matrix has run:
#        - `spawn_detached` in `src/_shared/proc.rs` calls a Unix API
#          (`process_group`) unconditionally;
#        - that file's `mod shell` test fixtures are now plain `echo`/`false`/
#          `sleep`, their `cmd /C` twin deleted;
#        - `tests/e2e.rs` chmods a `#!/bin/sh` shim through `PermissionsExt`.
#      None of the three blocks Linux; all three block Windows.
#
# **Nothing gates the platform at install time right now.** The `"os"`/`"cpu"`
# fields in the npm manifest were the third item on this list and the thing that
# refused the install off Apple Silicon; `distribution/01` deleted them, and the
# Homebrew formula that replaces them is `distribution/02`'s. Until it lands, a
# non-darwin:arm64 user is told nothing — a knowing window, recorded in that
# cycle's plan rather than patched over with a runtime host check.
#
# `printf`, not a `cat` heredoc: `cat` is shimmed to `bat` on the maintainer's
# machine and would inject ANSI decoration into the rows.
supported_targets() {
  printf '%s\n' \
    'aarch64-apple-darwin darwin arm64'
}

# How many binaries a complete release carries. One, today.
target_count() {
  supported_targets | wc -l | tr -d ' '
}

# The GitHub release asset filename for a target's os/cpu pair.
#
# Every target's binary is `claude-status` on disk, so they cannot all be
# uploaded to one release under that name. The os/cpu suffix is what makes them
# distinguishable, and it is the same `<os>-<cpu>` key the checksum manifest
# uses. Two consumers now name a target from it — the raw asset and the
# `.tar.gz` beside it — which is why the tarball derives its name here rather
# than spelling one out in the workflow.
asset_name() {
  printf 'claude-status-%s-%s' "$1" "$2"
}
